termdesc.patch - substrate's changes to Microsoft Word 3.0's terminal file.

/usr/lib/MSTOOLS/termdesc is where Word looks TERM up.  It lives inside
xenix286s.img, which is not tracked, so the changes made to it there are
kept here as a diff against the file as shipped in msw/word.img
(78163 bytes, sha256 6552409b008265abdeddae78a77e5522ed9dd7dd14ca43f27c5077941f5c06d7).
Applied, it gives the file in the image
(105781 bytes, sha256 5fc5aa20508073e1e0b84c6a289bd7ce5d39a7573072c420d1405ca16d45b345).

Two changes:

  1. The "ansi" entry is repaired (the first two hunks).  This is exactly
     what fix-termdesc-ansi.sh does to the shipped file; that script
     explains what was wrong with the entry and why each line is there.

  2. Five entries are added at the end: xterm (80x24), xterm-80x30,
     xterm-100x30, xterm-120x25 and xterm-132x20.  Word takes the screen
     size from the entry, so each size an xterm is run at needs its own.

tools/xenix/build-image.sh applies this when it builds the image.  By hand,
from the root of an extracted Word tree:

    patch -p1 < termdesc.patch

To refresh it after editing termdesc in the image:

    debugfs -R 'dump /usr/lib/MSTOOLS/termdesc termdesc.new' xenix286s.img
    tar -xOf msw/word.img ./usr/lib/MSTOOLS/termdesc > termdesc.orig
    diff -u --label a/usr/lib/MSTOOLS/termdesc --label b/usr/lib/MSTOOLS/termdesc \
        termdesc.orig termdesc.new

Some lines end in a space, as the shipped file's do: don't strip trailing
whitespace from this file.

